# `for (let n = first; n > 0; n = next(sum)) for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) sum ^= i`: the loop inside stands there twice, for an int32 and, rare, for any other number, behind one test of `n`.
# The loop around them stays as it is. Its copy would hold both forms again, and the test would stand where the loop is entered and where the next `n` is made in place of once
